> If something has an demand elasticity of "e", and I know that at price p0 it has demand d0, then what demand will it have at price p1?
**assistant**:
> To find the new demand \( d_1 \) at price \( p_1 \) given an initial demand \( d_0 \) at price \( p_0 \) and a demand elasticity \( e \), you can use the formula for elasticity:
> 
> \[
> e = \frac{\% \Delta \text{Quantity Demanded}}{\% \Delta \text{Price}}
> \]
> 
> Rearranging this formula gives:
> 
> \[
> \% \Delta \text{Quantity Demanded} = e \cdot \% \Delta \text{Price}
> \]
> 
> The percentage change in price is calculated as:
> 
> \[
> \% \Delta \text{Price} = \frac{p_1 - p_0}{p_0}
> \]
> 
> Now, apply this to find the change in demand:
> 
> \[
> \% \Delta \text{Quantity Demanded} = e \cdot \frac{p_1 - p_0}{p_0}
> \]
> 
> You can then calculate the new demand \( d_1 \) using:
> 
> \[
> d_1 = d_0 \cdot \left(1 + \% \Delta \text{Quantity Demanded} \right)
> \]
> 
> Substitute the expression for \(\% \Delta \text{Quantity Demanded}\) into this equation:
> 
> \[
> d_1 = d_0 \cdot \left(1 + e \cdot \frac{p_1 - p_0}{p_0}\right)
> \]
> 
> This provides you with the new demand at price \( p_1 \).
